# Release Approval Flow

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L0 core rule.

## Use This When

Any change is ready to leave its feature branch: merging to `dev`,
promoting stage, restarting services, or touching the live database.

## The Rule

1. **One epic — one branch — one landing.** All work of an epic, including
   every review fix that surfaces while it awaits approval, lands as
   commits on the SAME feature branch. Never spawn micro fix-branches for
   review feedback; they bloat history and dilute the epic.
2. **Validate on the isolated test stack** (units + integration + e2e).
   Real data only via a live clone restored into a non-live stack — never
   by touching live.
3. **Stand up the review stand — unprompted.** Before asking for
   approval, from the epic branch: `bun --cwd tests/e2e run
   test:e2e:stands`. One command stands up BOTH contours from the same
   commit — the test stack runs the suite off a `db reset`, and the review
   stack (`zero-memory-review`, server `:8789` / web `:3103`) comes up on a
   fresh clone of live with the branch's pending migrations applied, and
   stays up. Hand the reviewer the web URL; the owner signs in with LIVE
   credentials, since a physical clone carries the real accounts. Do not
   make them ask for this stand each time. `bun --cwd tests/e2e run
   stands:down` when it is no longer needed.

   **Which stand answers which question — the division is by what the data
   is, not by what is convenient.** The review stand carries a STABLE CLONE
   of production and is therefore the one to review on, to audit against,
   and to point a client at: it is the only contour where behaviour can be
   judged on real volume and real shapes. The dev and test stands are
   DEVELOPMENT contours whose databases get reformatted — `db reset` is
   routine there and destroys nothing anyone needs — so they answer "does
   the code run" and never "is the result any good".

   Two consequences worth stating, because both have cost time:
   - A branch carrying a MIGRATION can only be reviewed on real data here.
     Feature-branch DDL must never touch a stand that holds the corpus the
     deployment was migrated from, so the review stand's clone — where the
     launcher applies the branch's pending migrations as a rehearsal — is
     the only place the change and the data meet before promotion.
   - Reviewing on a reset-able stand answers a weaker question than it
     appears to. An empty corpus makes retrieval, ranking and anchoring
     look fine because there is nothing for them to get wrong.
4. **STOP at the approval gate.** Present the validated result and wait
   for the owner's explicit merge/promote command. A go-ahead to
   implement, in any wording, authorizes implementation only — it is never
   authorization to merge or promote. There is no "finalization"
   exemption.
5. **After approval — the feature's path to production, and what is tested
   where.** Each step tests the thing that is new at that step; testing the
   same combination twice is waste, and skipping the step where a NEW
   combination first exists is the actual risk.

   a. **Cut the branch from `main`.** It is stable by definition.

   b. **On the feature branch — the gates, on a COMMITTED tip:** `check`
      (format, lint, typecheck, env templates), units, and the FULL e2e.
      A run against an uncommitted working tree proves a tree no commit
      points at; name the HEAD a run was made on, or it is not evidence.

   c. **Merge into `dev` — only while OTHER work is in flight.** `dev` is
      the compatibility contour: it exists to be the one place where this
      feature meets the others, so it is the only place that combination
      has ever existed. With two or more features in flight, merge into
      `dev` and run the FULL e2e there on the committed merge; conflicts
      are resolved on `dev`, not later. With a single feature in flight
      there is nothing to integrate against — the branch run in (b) IS the
      integration run, and `dev` is not used.

   d. **Squash the feature branch onto `main` — never a merge commit
      there.** One commit per epic, subject in the usual one-line form, and
      the branch it came from recorded as a trailer in a SECOND `-m`, so
      the subject stays one line:

      ```
      git merge --squash <branch>
      git commit -m "<type(scope): subject>" \
                 -m "Squashed-from: <branch> (<short-sha>)"
      ```

      The feature branch SURVIVES its landing: it is the only remaining
      carrier of how the work got there, step by step. Deleting a landed
      branch is the owner's decision, never routine cleanup. No test run is
      owed at this step — `main` gains exactly the tree that (b), and (c)
      where it applied, already proved.

   e. **Release** — `run.sh sync` re-runs check, units and the full e2e
      before it cuts the tag, so the published tree is proved once more at
      the moment of publication.

   When `dev` is in use it carries merge commits while `main` carries one
   squash per epic, so the two drift apart permanently. That is the model
   working, not a defect: do not try to make one an ancestor of the
   other, and do not "repair" the divergence.

   On `main` an epic is ONE commit, so `git revert <squash>` takes it back
   out whole, and `git log --grep='Squashed-from: <branch>'` finds where a
   branch landed. `merge.ff=false`, set in the repository config by
   `scripts/setup-git-merge-policy.sh` (run by `prepare`/`postinstall`),
   still guards the `dev` merges against git's silent fast-forward; a
   squash is not affected by it.

   Applying a schema change to a serving deployment and any data one-shots
   there are the owner's manual steps — hand over exact commands, and
   rehearse a migration before proposing it rather than overriding a
   refusal.
6. **Post-approval follow-ups** go through the same cycle as a new
   deliberate branch (batched, not one branch per tweak). Applied
   migrations are never edited — forward migrations only; never roll the
   database back for code-level fixes.

## Why

The live stack serves the owner's real memory; the owner reviews before
anything reaches it. Two epics were once merged and promoted without
approval by over-reading a go-ahead — this rule is the fix, settled
deliberately; do not re-litigate it per session.
